inetics during controlled rolling. [Data: Entities (116)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Strain-assisted transformation links deformation to phase change\",\"explanation\":\"STRAIN-ASSISTED Γ TO Α TRANSFORMATION is described as a phase transformation in dual-phase steel that occurs when deformation is applied in the non-recrystallization region. The transformation begins from the γ, or austenite, phase. [Data: Entities (1017); Relationships (1419)]\\n\\nThe applied strain shifts transformation behavior by increasing the α-transformation temperature and decreasing the martensite transformation temperature. This indicates that deformation does not merely shape the existing microstructure; it also influences the temperatures and pathways at which phase transformation occurs. [Data: Entities (1017)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Deformation finishing determines the resulting ferrite structure\",\"explanation\":\"DEFORMATION FINISHING represents the stage at which deformation is completed in the austenite or austenite–ferrite region. The finishing location determines whether the resulting structure contains fine-grained polygonal ferrite or polygonal ferrite grains and subgrains. [Data: Entities (1103)]\\n\\nA related relationship states that finishing deformation in the AUSTENITE REGION produces fine-grained polygonal ferrite. This makes the finishing stage a key control point for the final grain morphology. [Data: Entities (1104); Relationships (1589)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Accelerated cooling follows the deformation stage\",\"explanation\":\"ACCELERATED COOLING AFTER DEFORMATION is a defined process stage in which accelerated cooling is applied after deformation during controlled rolling of thick plates. Its relationship to DEFORMATION explicitly places cooling after the deformation stage. [Data: Entities (1172); Relationships (1701)]\\n\\nThe supplied data does not specify the exact cooling rate, temperature schedule, or resulting phases. It does, however, establish accelerated cooling as a dist